---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { editorialPolicy } from "../lib/category-editorial";
import { pageGraphSchema } from "../lib/seo";
import { siteConfig } from "../lib/site";

const path = "/editorial-standards";
const title = `Editorial standards | ${siteConfig.name}`;
const pageName = "Editorial standards";
const description =
  "How agentfirst.directory researches, reviews, classifies, orders, corrects, and removes directory listings.";
const contentRepoUrl = "https://github.com/bradvin/agentfirst.directory";
const pullRequestsUrl = `${contentRepoUrl}/pulls`;

const structuredData = [
  pageGraphSchema({
    path,
    name: pageName,
    description,
    type: "WebPage",
    datePublished: editorialPolicy.effectiveDate,
    dateModified: editorialPolicy.effectiveDate,
    lastReviewed: editorialPolicy.effectiveDate,
    breadcrumbs: [
      { name: siteConfig.shortName, path: "/" },
      { name: pageName, path },
    ],
  }),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath={path}
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <div class="shell-inner policy-shell">
    <section class="page-intro page-intro-centered">
      <p class="detail-overline">
        Editorial policy {editorialPolicy.version} · Effective {editorialPolicy.effectiveDateLabel}
      </p>
      <h1 class="page-title">How listings are researched and reviewed</h1>
      <p class="page-copy">
        The directory applies one public inclusion test to vendor submissions, community contributions, and
        maintainer-authored entries. Passing that test establishes taxonomy fit; it does not establish product quality.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel">
      <p class="detail-overline">Evidence standard</p>
      <h2>Material claims need a maintained first-party source</h2>
      <ol class="policy-test-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Prefer the closest source.</strong> Product documentation, protocol specifications, release notes,
          source repositories, licence files, and official pricing pages take priority over summaries and directories.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Match evidence to the claim.</strong> A homepage mention of “agents” does not support detailed claims
          about permissions, interfaces, deployment, pricing, or interoperability.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Separate fact from positioning.</strong> Vendor terminology may be reported with attribution, but it
          is not converted into an independent performance or quality judgment.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Use secondary sources only as context.</strong> Third-party coverage may help find a lead or explain
          a market, but it cannot replace first-party evidence for inclusion.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-classification-grid" aria-label="Editorial review stages">
      <article class="detail-panel policy-classification-card">
        <p class="detail-overline">1 · Scope</p>
        <h3>Identify the agent role</h3>
        <p>
          The reviewer states what an agent, agent builder, or interoperable agent system can do with the product or
          protocol, and why that capability is material.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-panel policy-classification-card">
        <p class="detail-overline">2 · Verify</p>
        <h3>Check the specific evidence</h3>
        <p>
          The reviewer checks maintained first-party material and rejects thin integrations, unsupported marketing,
          and generic technical compatibility.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-panel policy-classification-card">
        <p class="detail-overline">3 · Classify</p>
        <h3>Choose one defensible class</h3>
        <p>
          Each accepted listing receives the most specific supported classification. Classification is not a rating,
          award, recommendation, or substitute for due diligence.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel">
      <p class="detail-overline">Experience and verification</p>
      <h2>Documentation review is not hands-on testing</h2>
      <p>
        Unless a listing explicitly describes a dated test and its conditions, it should be read as a review of public
        documentation, not evidence that the directory installed, purchased, benchmarked, or security-tested the tool.
        Superlatives and comparative claims require a disclosed method and supporting evidence; otherwise they are not
        editorial conclusions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel">
      <p class="detail-overline">Review cadence</p>
      <h2>Review happens at submission and when material change is found</h2>
      <p>
        Every proposed listing is reviewed when it enters the directory. Existing entries are rechecked when a reader,
        vendor, contributor, or maintainer reports a material change or correction. The directory does not currently
        promise a fixed recurring re-review interval, so fast-changing facts such as price, availability, limits, and
        licence terms should be confirmed on the linked first-party source before a decision is made.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel">
      <p class="detail-overline">Conflicts, sponsorship, and affiliate links</p>
      <h2>Commercial relationships do not change the inclusion test</h2>
      <ul class="policy-test-list">
        <li>Contributors should disclose employment, ownership, investment, or another material vendor relationship in the pull request.</li>
        <li>A maintainer relationship to a listed tool does not exempt the entry from evidence and classification requirements.</li>
        <li>The directory does not currently sell rankings or sponsored placement.</li>
        <li>Outbound listing links are not currently paid affiliate links. Any future compensated link or placement must be clearly labelled.</li>
        <li>Advertising or sponsorship, if introduced, will not guarantee acceptance and will not alter editorial classification.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel">
      <p class="detail-overline">Ordering</p>
      <h2>Position is not a score</h2>
      <p>
        Homepage listings are shuffled. Category and tool ordering can use an explicit maintenance value and otherwise
        falls back to readable label or name ordering. Placement does not represent quality, popularity, payment,
        market share, or editorial preference. Any future ranked recommendation will require a separate published
        methodology and will be labelled as a ranking.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel">
      <p class="detail-overline">Public record</p>
      <h2>Changes are proposed in public</h2>
      <p>
        Directory content is stored in a{" "}
        <a class="detail-link" href={contentRepoUrl} rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">public GitHub repository</a>.
        Proposed additions and material edits can be examined in{" "}
        <a class="detail-link" href={pullRequestsUrl} rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">pull requests</a>, including
        the contributor identity and discussion available there. Public history is useful provenance, but submitter
        identity is not the same as editorial authorship or independent verification.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel">
      <p class="detail-overline">Corrections, appeals, and removals</p>
      <h2>Stronger evidence can change a decision</h2>
      <p>
        A reader or vendor may challenge a fact, classification, rejection, or removal with a specific first-party
        source. The maintainer may correct the wording, add a caveat, reclassify, temporarily unpublish, or remove an
        entry when evidence no longer supports it. A vendor request alone does not require publication or deletion of
        accurate public-interest information. The full process is documented in the{" "}
        <a class="detail-link" href="/corrections">corrections policy</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel" id="data-reuse">
      <p class="detail-overline">Content and data rights</p>
      <h2>No separate bulk-reuse licence has been declared</h2>
      <p>
        The repositories and live JSON/CSV exports are public for inspection, citation, and contribution, but their
        availability does not grant a separate open content or compiled-data licence for bulk republication. Link to
        the canonical page and attribute short quotations. Product names, logos, documentation, and other third-party
        materials remain subject to their owners' terms. Any future immutable research release will state explicit
        reuse terms next to that release.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-cta">
      <p>Want to inspect or improve the evidence behind a listing?</p>
      <a class="button button-primary" href="/submit">Read the contribution guide</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
